<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Paine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1737">1737</a>–<a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Thomas Pain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Paine was an English-born American Founding Father, political philosopher and statesman whose pamphlets Common Sense (1776) and The American Crisis (1776–1783) helped inspire the colonists to declare independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thetford">Thetford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, he came to the British American colonies in 1774 with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>. His Rights of Man (1791), a defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>, led to his conviction in absentia for seditious libel in 1792. Elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">French National Convention</a>, he was held in the Luxembourg Prison from December 1793 to November 1794, and in The Age of Reason he advocated Deism and argued against Christian doctrine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-paine.jpg" alt="Thomas Paine" width="330" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurent Dabos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurent_Dabos_–_Thomas_Paine_–_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>(1789) the Abbé Sieyès attacked the French nobility as a privileged caste living off the nation, and in Rights of Man (1791) <b>Thomas Paine</b> called the idea of hereditary legislators “as absurd as an hereditary mathematician”.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Paine</b>’s Common Sense (1776) denounced hereditary succession as an imposition on posterity and declared one honest man worth more to society “than all the crowned ruffians that ever lived”; in Rights of Man (1791) he argued that the idea of hereditary legislators was as absurd as that…</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Marx rejected the foundations of liberal theory, and Vladimir Lenin held that liberal science defends wage slavery, although liberals such as <b>Thomas Paine</b> also criticised wage slavery.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>The first pamphlet of <b>Thomas Paine</b>'s series The American Crisis appeared in The Pennsylvania Journal, signed &quot;Common Sense&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-19">19 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
